        "tapescript": "PART 1 – (Questions 1–5) A museum curator speaking to student volunteers about a heritage evening\nCurator: Good afternoon, everyone, and thank you for giving up your Saturday to attend this induction session. As you know, our annual ‘Night at the City Museum’ is coming up next month, and your role as student stewards will be vital.\nFirst, a quick update on the schedule. If you picked up one of the early flyers in the university library, you’ll have seen Friday the fourteenth of November printed on the cover. Unfortunately, the city council is carrying out essential electrical maintenance along Museum Street that evening, so we’ve had to shift the event by twenty-four hours to Saturday the fifteenth. A few colleagues asked whether Sunday the sixteenth might be calmer, but our visiting conservationists from Florence are flying home first thing that morning, so Saturday is now set in stone.\nNow, a word on crowd management, which is where last year’s feedback really opened our eyes. We welcomed nearly eighteen hundred visitors last November, and because the event is funded by a heritage grant, charging an admission fee is out of the question. Some of you suggested keeping the galleries open until satu—until one in the morning, but our municipal licence strictly forbids anything past midnight. What the visitor questionnaires actually showed was that people loved the exhibits, yet nearly forty percent complained about severe bottlenecks around the grand staircase during the first hour. That means our top priority this year is guiding arrivals along staggered routes through the side wings rather than letting everyone surge into the main hall at once.\nOne room where you’ll need to be especially vigilant is the ‘Restoration Live’ studio on the second floor. Inside, two specialists will be cleaning eighteenth-century seascapes in front of small groups of twelve. Now, don’t worry about chemical fumes—they switched to odourless, plant-based solvents years ago—and visitors are welcome to take photographs provided their flash is switched off. The catch is the room’s microclimate. The canvases are extremely sensitive, and if the heavy glass doors are propped open for more than half a minute, the humidity inside shoots up and triggers the conservation alarm. So please make sure each group enters and exits promptly.\nDownstairs in the courtyard, we’re bringing back the Roman Coin Minting stall. Teenagers, in particular, flock to it. It’s not the fastest activity—the queue rarely drops below twenty minutes—and at two pounds a go to cover the pewter blanks, it’s actually our only paid activity. What makes it irresistible to secondary-school kids is that, after striking the Roman emblem, they get to stamp their own initials on the reverse side and take the coin home as a keepsake.\nFinancially, that one stall is a little goldmine. Last year, after we deducted eighty pounds for the raw pewter discs, it handed over a clear profit of two hundred and forty pounds to our education fund. Let’s hope we match that this year!",
