        "tapescript": "PART 1 – (Questions 1–5) A botanical garden director speaking to volunteer guides about an autumn orchard festival\nMr Evans: Good morning, everyone. Do grab a cup of tea and take a seat. Thank you all for volunteering to help run this year’s Autumn Apple and Orchard Festival.\nLet’s start with the date, as there’s been a slight mix-up in the local press. The parish magazine printed Saturday the fourth of October, which was our provisional booking back in the spring. However, the national bee-keeping association is holding its annual conference in our main lecture theatre that weekend, so we’ve pushed the festival back to Sunday the twelfth. Some of you asked whether Saturday the eleventh would work better, but the marquee erectors can only set up the stalls on Saturday afternoon, so Sunday is our only viable option.\nNow, a quick look at the finances and what last year’s numbers tell us. Our target is to raise four thousand pounds towards restoring the Victorian glasshouse. Last autumn, gate receipts brought in nearly two thousand, which was right on target. Several committee members suggested raising the adult entry fee from five pounds to seven, but we know from experience that higher gate prices deter young families. Nor are we going to charge for car parking, as that would cause chaos on the narrow country lane outside. What last year’s accounts made crystal clear is that visitors spend generously once they’re inside if there are hands-on tastings and workshops, so expanding those paid activities is how we’ll bridge the funding gap this year.\nOne highlight you’ll be supervising is the Heritage Apple Identification table in the walled garden. Guests bring windfall apples from their own gardens for our pomologists to name. Now, people often assume the experts struggle because there are over four hundred rare varieties in the county, or because the apples are bruised. Actually, our team can spot a ribston pippin at ten paces. The real headache is that amateurs bring in a single apple picked from the shady side of the tree, without any leaves or a twig attached, which makes a definitive match almost impossible.\nOver on the orchard lawn, we’re running the Artisan Cider-Pressing workshop again, where children crank the wooden press and bottle their own fresh apple juice. It’s always packed with primary-school kids. It’s certainly not the quickest stall—each batch takes fifteen minutes—and at one pound fifty a bottle, it’s pricier than the face-painting booth. I’m convinced its appeal lies in the fact that the children get to wax-seal the cork themselves and paste a personalised label with their name on the glass before taking it home.\nLast year, that single press netted a tidy one hundred and eighty pounds for the glasshouse fund after we’d deducted sixty pounds for the empty glass bottles and corks. Let’s see if we can top that on Sunday!",
