        "tapescript": "PART 1 – (Questions 1–5) A national park ranger speaking to trail marshals about a charity night hike\nMr Daniel: Evening, everyone. Come inside the visitor centre barn and shut the door. Thanks for volunteering as trail marshals for our annual Moorland Charity Night Hike.\nFirst, let’s confirm the date, as there’s been a change since the registration packs went out. The printed sponsorship forms stated Friday the twenty-fourth of October. However, the forestry commission is hauling felled timber along the lower fire-track that Friday evening, so we’ve moved the hike to Saturday the twenty-fifth. A few marshals asked whether Sunday the twenty-sixth would be quieter, but the last rural bus leaves the valley at nine o’clock on Sunday nights, which would strand hikers who don’t drive, so Saturday the twenty-fifth is locked in.\nNow, a quick look at our fundraising target for mountain-rescue stretchers. Last autumn we aimed for four thousand pounds and came up five hundred pounds short. Walker registration fees brought in two thousand six hundred pounds, which was our safety limit of two hundred hikers—we can’t allow larger numbers onto the peat bog at night. Nor do we want to raise the registration fee, as that would put off university hiking clubs. That means our base-camp stalls at the finish line must bring in that extra five hundred pounds this year.\nFor those of you stationed at Checkpoint Three on the ridge, you’ll be supervising the compass-bearing challenge. Hikers have to take a night bearing across the valley to a flashing beacon. People assume walkers get it wrong because the wind shakes their hands, or because the map contours are hard to read by head-torch. Actually, the ridge is sheltered by a stone wall and our maps are printed at huge scale. The real trap is the iron-rich basalt rock in that outcrop, which deflects a magnetic compass needle by up to eight degrees unless hikers step onto the wooden boardwalk first.\nBack at the base-camp barn, we’re running the Reflective Dog-Tag Engraving stall, which is a huge hit with hikers and their families. At two pounds a tag, it’s the same price as a mug of soup, and there’s always a queue by the engraving machine. What makes it so popular with younger walkers is that, whether they finish the full ten-mile loop or just the short family trail, we engrave their exact finish time and altitude onto a glow-in-the-dark aluminium tag for their rucksack.\nLast year, that engraving stall cleared two hundred and twenty pounds in profit after we’d subtracted sixty pounds for the blank aluminium tags and split rings. Right, let’s check your radios!",
