        "tapescript": "PART 1 – (Questions 1–5) A glasshouse curator speaking to stewards about a tropical butterfly and orchid evening\nMrs Victoria: Good afternoon, everyone. Come into the potting shed where it’s cooler. Thank you all for volunteering to steward our annual Rare Orchid and Tropical Butterfly Evening.\nFirst, let’s double-check the date. Our spring brochure listed Friday the seventeenth of July. However, our shipment of Costa Rican butterfly chrysalises was delayed in customs by two days, and they won’t emerge from their pupae until Saturday morning, so we’ve moved the event to Saturday the eighteenth. A few stewards asked whether Sunday the nineteenth would be safer, but the glasshouse misting system is scheduled for its annual descaling flush all day Sunday, so Saturday the eighteenth is final.\nNow, a word on our fundraising target for the new rainwater-harvesting cistern. Last summer we aimed to raise six thousand pounds and finished eight hundred pounds short. Timed-entry tickets brought in four thousand pounds, which was the maximum capacity fire regulations allow inside the glasshouse walkways. We don’t want to hike ticket prices, as that would exclude local students, and we certainly won’t charge visitors extra to enter the butterfly emergence zone. What last year’s numbers showed is that visitors love hands-on botanical workshops on the terrace, so those terrace stalls must bring in the extra eight hundred pounds this year.\nTwo of you will be stationed at theVanilla Hand-Pollination demonstration table inside the humid zone. Visitors use a bamboo toothpick to transfer pollen inside a delicate orchid blossom. People always assume beginners fail because their hands tremble, or because the pollen grain is too tiny to see without a magnifying glass. Actually, the pollen mass is bright yellow and easy to spot. The real difficulty is a tiny flap of tissue called the rostellum that separates the male and female parts; unless you lift that flap gently with the tip of the toothpick first, the pollen simply won’t stick.\nOut on the terrace, we’re running the Mini-Terrarium Jar Bar, which is a huge favourite with teenagers and young adults. At five pounds a jar, it’s our priciest activity, and there’s always a queue along the balustrade. Why is it so popular? Because even if someone has never kept a houseplant alive before, our horticulturists seal a self-watering layer ofvolcanic pebbles and live cushion moss inside a corked apothecary jar, so nobody goes home without a maintenance-free miniature ecosystem.\nLast year, that Terrarium Bar made a clear profit of three hundred and sixty pounds after we’d deducted ninety pounds for the glass jars, pebbles and corks. Let’s go and check the airlocks!",
