        "tapescript": "PART 2 – (Questions 6–10) A radio report about a wave-powereddesalination buoy called AquaBuoy\nPresenter: Good morning.In today’s coastal innovation segment, we travel to theScottish Hebrides, where engineers at Caledonia Marine haveanchored AquaBuoy—an offshore buoy that uses ocean swells toturn seawater into drinking water.\nChief engineer Mr DanielFraser says the team had a very specific target in mind.\nMrDaniel Fraser: “People see a yellow buoy bobbing half akilometre offshore and assume we’re generating electricity forthe mainland grid, or acting as a navigation beacon forferries. In fact, there isn’t a single electrical generatorinside. Remote islands pay exorbitant sums to ship in dieseljust to run onshore desalination pumps, and our mission was todeliver clean tap water using raw wave motion alone.”\nPresenter: Inside the buoy, the rise and fall of each wavedrives a hydraulic piston that forces seawater through areverse-osmosis membrane, sending fresh water ashore througha flexible seabed pipe. During normal swells, the buoy floatson the surface. However, an accelerometer tracks wave height,and whenever storm waves exceed six metres, a ballast chamberfloods automatically, winching the buoy three metres beneaththe surface where the water is calmer.\nBecause seawater isfull of microscopic algae, filters normally clog within weeks.AquaBuoy avoids chemical cleaners by using a copper-nickelintake grille. The alloy does two things at once: it preventsbarnacles and algae from latching on, and it pre-strainscoarse sand before the water ever reaches the delicatemembrane.\nInterestingly, the flexible high-pressure seabedpipe was supplied by a Norwegian firm outside the waterindustry. The company doesn’t make reverse-osmosis membranesor marine anchors; instead, it specialises in braided Kevlarhoses for deep-sea hydraulic rescue tools—ideal for survivingrocky seabeds without chafing.\nSo, will Mediterranean andCaribbean islands soon order AquaBuoy in bulk? Mr Fraser iscautious.\nMr Daniel Fraser: “Island councils love the zerofuel bill, but our current buoy needs swells of at least oneand a half metres. Until we finish testing our low-wave gearratio next spring, sales will be confined to exposed Atlanticcoasts.”",
