        "tapescript": "PART 3 – (Questions 11–15) An interview with Ruth Calloway, manager of a regional orchestra\nInterviewer: Ruth Calloway, welcome. You manage a regional orchestra, which isn’t an obvious career. How did you end up there?\nRuth: By a slightly roundabout route. I did a music degree and then, to the surprise of my tutors, an evening diploma in business. There wasn’t such a thing as a course in arts management in those days, so I had to put the two together myself. At the time it looked an odd combination, but I’m glad I did it because, as I tell my younger colleagues, you can’t run an orchestra on enthusiasm alone.\nInterviewer: Orchestras rely on star soloists, don’t they? Does that make your life difficult?\nRuth: Do I mind? Honestly, no. They can be demanding. One pianist asked for the whole programme to be changed a week before the concert, and yes, that caused chaos. But a famous name fills the hall, and a full hall pays for the rest of the season. Their fees aren’t small, but it’s a price worth paying, and I’d pay it again.\nInterviewer: What’s your favourite part of a concert day?\nRuth: People assume it’s the moment the audience arrives, and the buzz is lovely, but it’s also chaos. During the performance I’m usually at the back with a clipboard, so I’m not really listening either. And I don’t mind firefighting, that’s the job, but it isn’t what I love. My favourite moment comes at about five o’clock, when the chairs are set, the lights are right and the hall is completely silent. I stand at the back and think, right, it’s all been worth it.\nInterviewer: Not every day goes to plan, though. Last winter you had to cancel a concert.\nRuth: Yes. The forecast promised heavy snow, so I cancelled at lunchtime. In the end only a dusting fell, and by seven o’clock the roads were perfectly clear. I kept asking myself whether I’d panicked. Everyone said better safe than sorry, and I know they’re right, but I felt no relief at all. I can’t blame the forecasters, either: they gave a warning, and I made the decision.\nInterviewer: What did it cost you?\nRuth: We refunded everybody and lost roughly a third of that month’s income. We didn’t let anyone go, and we’ve kept ticket prices where they were. But we have had to put off buying the new set of timpani we’d been saving for, and the players are still using the old ones.",
