        "tapescript": "PART 3 – (Questions 11–15) An interview with Clara Vance, a forensic book conservator\nInterviewer: My guest today is Clara Vance, a forensic book conservator who restores centuries-old manuscripts. Clara, it’s a fascinating niche. How did you get into it?\nClara: Almost by accident. I completed a degree in analytical chemistry, and my department head assumed I’d go into pharmaceuticals. Instead, I spent two years apprenticed to an old-school master bookbinder in Edinburgh. My university tutors thought I was throwing my scientific training away, and at first the two worlds felt miles apart. Yet knowing how pigments và parchment degrade at a molecular level while having the manual dexterity to sew a sixteenth-century spine turned out to be the exact blend modern conservation demands.\nInterviewer: Your studio works for national libraries, but also for wealthy private collectors. How do you find dealing with private clients?\nClara: It’s a balancing act. Occasionally a collector will buy a stained medieval prayer book at auction and ask me to bleach the pages snow-white so it looks brand new in their display case—which I politely but firmly refuse to do. And yes, they can be impatient. But private commissions pay generous rates, and that surplus is what allows my studio to restore parish records for cash-strapped local archives free of charge. So I’m more than happy to accommodate them.\nInterviewer: When you’re spending months on a single volume, what’s the most rewarding moment?\nClara: Outsiders imagine it’s the glamour of unveiling the finished manuscript at a press launch, or stumbling across the autograph of a famous monarch. Those are nice, of course, but they’re rare. What genuinely thrills me is when I’m working under raking light and spot a faint shopping list, a pressed wildflower, or a doodle in the margin left by an ordinary seventeenth-century apprentice. Suddenly, a museum object becomes a human conversation across centuries.\nInterviewer: You’ve also dealt with emergencies. Last autumn, a county archive suffered a serious flood.\nClara: Yes, a heating pipe burst on a Sunday night. My team worked thirty-six hours straight to pack four hundred soaked ledgers into blast freezers before mould could set in. The county council sent us a glowing letter of thanks, and we saved over ninety percent of the collection. Everyone expected me to feel proud, or at least relieved. Honestly, though, I was seething. Six months earlier, during a routine survey, I’d flagged that exact corroded pipe in writing, and the facilities manager had brushed it off as too expensive to replace.\nInterviewer: Has that experience changed how your studio operates?\nClara: Completely. We haven’t put up our emergency call-out fees, and we still take on small rural museums. However, we’ve introduced a strict clause: before we sign any multi-year conservation retainer with an institution, their premises must pass an independent plumbing and fire-safety audit. We simply won’t invest hundreds of hours restoring treasures only to see them ruined by a preventable leak.",
