        "tapescript": "PART 3 – (Questions 11–15) An interview with Oliver Finch, aheritage seed-bank curator\nInterviewer: My guest today isOliver Finch, curator of the National Heritage Seed Vault.Oliver, preserving thousands of rare vegetable and grainvarieties sounds like a very specialised calling. How did youtrain for it?\nOliver: By stitching together two fields thatnobody else thought belonged on the same CV. I did anundergraduate degree in plant genetics, and then—much to myacademic supervisor’s bafflement—spent a year earning aprofessional certificate in commercial cold-chain logistics.There was no university course in seed-vault curation backthen. On paper it looked bizarre, yet keeping fifty thousandseed lots viable at minus twenty degrees is half botany andhalf warehouse engineering.\nInterviewer: You supply seeds toacademic researchers, but also to trendy Michelin-starredchefs who want heirloom vegetables. Are the chefs difficultto deal with?\nOliver: They can certainly be demanding! Lastspring, a famous chef rang up demanding two kilos of asixteenth-century purple carrot by Friday—not realising weonly hold three hundred seeds of each variety to germinate,not sacks of produce. So I have to explain our rulespatiently. All the same, when a top restaurant puts aforgotten local bean on its menu and credits our vault, publicdonations triple overnight, which pays for our ruralcollection trips. So I welcome their calls.\nInterviewer:What’s the most satisfying part of your working week?\nOliver:Visitors assume it’s walking into the sub-zero vault in athermal parka, or hosting foreign agriculture ministers. Intruth, the vault is freezing and noisy with fans. What Igenuinely live for is Tuesday mornings in the germinationglasshouse, when a seed packet collected from a demolishedfarmstead forty years ago finally pushes up its first tinygreen shoot. Seeing a variety wake up after four decades ofsleep never gets old.\nInterviewer: You’ve had some nerve-wrackingmoments, too. Two summers ago, a wildfire came close to yoursecondary field station.\nOliver: It did. The fire serviceordered an evacuation at noon, and we loaded our backup seedtrays into refrigerated vans in forty minutes flat. In theend, the wind shifted and the flames stopped two miles shortof the boundary fence. My board of trustees praised our rapidresponse and expected me to breathe a huge sigh of relief.Instead, I lay awake all night furious with myself, because Irealised that if the main access lane had been blocked byfallen trees, our vans wouldn’t have had a second exitroute.\nInterviewer: What changed after that near miss?\nOliver: We didn’t relocate the station, and we haven’t cutback on our summer growing trials. What we have done isduplicate our entire living collection at a sister institutethree hundred miles north in Scotland, so no single regionaldisaster can ever threaten a lineage again.",
