        "tapescript": "PART 3 – (Questions 11–15) An interview with ArthurPendelton, a master stained-glass conservator\nInterviewer:Arthur Pendelton, welcome. You spend your days high up oncathedral scaffolding conserving medieval stained-glasswindows. How did you get into such a specialised craft?\nArthur: By pairing two subjects that my art-school friendsthought belonged on different planets. I did a degree infine-art portraiture, and then enrolled in a postgraduatediploma in inorganic glass chemistry at Sheffield. Mypainting tutors thought I’d gone over to the dark side. Yetwhen you’re conserving a fourteenth-century cathedral window,you need an artist’s eye to read the medieval brushwork and achemist’s grasp of how potassium ions leach out of potashglass in acid rain.\nInterviewer: Alongside cathedrals, yourworkshop takes commissions from celebrity homeowners buyingVictorian mansions. Are private clients hard to please?\nArthur: They can be entertainingly quirky. One rock musicianasked us to insert his electric guitar into a Victoriancoat-of-arms landing window—which we did, on a removablepanel so the original heritage glass wasn’t altered! And yes,their architects always want everything finished yesterday.But private mansion projects pay commercial rates, and thatsurplus lets us subsidise apprentice wages so youngcraftspeople can spend five years learning medieval leadingtechniques.\nInterviewer: What’s the most special moment ofa restoration project?\nArthur: People imagine it’s therededication service when the bishop blesses the window infront of television cameras. Actually, during ceremonies I’mjust squinting up to check none of the mortar joints areleaking. For me, nothing beats holding a freshly cleanedfourteenth-century glass face on my light-table and spottingthe tiny fingerprint left in the wet enamel by the medievalmonk who painted it six hundred years ago.\nInterviewer:You’ve had some close calls too. Last winter, a gale toreloose part of the temporary sheeting on a cathedral toweryou were working on.\nArthur: Yes. A gust ripped a tarpaulinpanel at two in the morning while three bays of medievaltracery were empty. Thankfully, the panels themselves werelocked in our ground-floor workshop, and the stone framessuffered zero damage. The cathedral dean praised our team forsecuring the tarpaulin by dawn, and everyone said no harmwas done. Yet I felt sick to my stomach for days, because ifone of the loose scaffold clips had blown through theadjacent choir window, we’d have destroyed pricelessfifteenth-century glass.\nInterviewer: How did that nightchange your procedures?\nArthur: We haven’t stopped workingthrough the winter, and we haven’t raised our insurancesurcharges. Instead, whenever we remove an original windowpanel now, we bolt a rigid polycarbonate storm-shielddirectly into the stone rebate within the same hour—we neverrely on flexible tarpaulins overnight anymore.",
