        "tapescript": "PART 3 – (Questions 11–15) An interview with George Vance, anolfactory historian\nInterviewer: George Vance, welcome. Youare an olfactory historian—you reconstruct the smells ofpast centuries, from Roman spice markets to eighteenth-centuryapothecaries, for museum exhibitions. How on earth did youtrain for that?\nGeorge: By bolting together twoworlds that rarely speak to each other. I completed a degreein Renaissance cultural history, and then went to Grasse insouthern France for two years to train in organic fragrancecompounding. My history professors thought I’d abandonedscholarship for the luxury cosmetics counter. Yet torecreate a sixteenth-century plague doctor’s pomander, youhave to read Latin guild recipes in the morning and balancevolatile terpene molecules in a fume cupboard in theafternoon.\nInterviewer: Theme parks and immersive theatreproducers also hire you. How do you find working withentertainment clients?\nGeorge: They’re great fun, even ifthey always want to turn the volume up to eleven! A theatredirector once asked me to make an auditorium smell of aVictorian London tannery at full strength—I had to warn himthat authentic tannery smell would clear the theatre inthirty seconds flat! So we created a subtle leather-and-birch-taraccord instead. Commercial theatre fees are generous, andthey pay for the gas-chromatography equipment we use toanalyse residue inside ancient Egyptian perfume jars forpublic museums.\nInterviewer: What’s the most rewardingmoment when an exhibition opens?\nGeorge: People think it’sreading rave reviews from art critics in the Sundaynewspapers. Honestly, critics mostly write about the visualdisplays anyway. What moves me is standing quietly by ascent-diffuser column in a local history gallery and watchingan elderly visitor catch a whiff of coal-tar soap ordried lavender and suddenly light up, telling theirgrandchild a vivid childhood memory they hadn’t thought aboutin sixty years.\nInterviewer: Has a scent installation evergone wrong?\nGeorge: Painfully so. Two years ago, at amaritime museum, a timer relay stuck on a diffuser releasingthe aroma of smoked Baltic herring. By ten in the morning,the gift shop next door smelled like a kipper smokehouse.The museum director laughed it off—in fact, sales ofseaside cookbooks in the shop doubled that day! Everyonetold me it was a harmless quirk. Internally, though, I washorrified, because concentrated essential oils can triggerasthma attacks in sensitive visitors if ventilation limitsare exceeded.\nInterviewer: What safeguard did you put inplace after the herring incident?\nGeorge: We haven’t stoppedusing food aromas, and we haven’t reduced the number ofdiffusers in galleries. Instead, every diffuser nozzle isnow fitted with a mechanical gravity-valve and a miniatureair-quality sensor that physically cuts the airflow ifairborne concentration rises above five parts per million.",
