        "tapescript": "PART 3 – (Questions 11–15) An interview with HazelVance-Sterling, an astrophotography archive curator\nInterviewer: Hazel Vance-Sterling, welcome. You oversee avault of half a million nineteenth- and early twentieth-centuryglass photographic negatives of the night sky, digitisingthem for modern astronomers. How did you come to do thisjob?\nHazel: Through a combination that raised eyebrows inboth departments. I did my first degree in observationalastrophysics, and then took a two-year fellowship innineteenth-century silver-gelatin photographic conservation.My astronomy professors thought I was retreating into history.Yet if you want to scan a glass plate taken in 1895 withoutpeeling off the fragile emulsion, and then calibrate thestar positions to arc-second accuracy, you need both craftsin equal measure.\nInterviewer: Sci-fi film designers andcoffee-table book publishers often ask to reproduce yourvintage star plates. Do you mind dealing with commercialpublishers?\nHazel: Not at all, though their requests can befunny. One book designer asked if we could digitally rotatethe Orion Nebula by ninety degrees and boost the magentasaturation on an 1888 monochrome plate so it would fit aportrait book cover! I politely explained that archivalintegrity comes first. Even so, licensing high-resolutionscans to publishers and planetarium shows brings in vitalrevenue that pays for our custom acid-free paper sleeves, soI’m always glad to help them.\nInterviewer: What’s the mostthrilling part of your day-to-day work?\nHazel: Visitorsassume it’s operating our custom laser-table scanner orco-authoring papers on supernovae. The scanner is impressive,certainly. But what truly thrills me is examining the glassmargins under a loupe and reading the tiny fountain-pennotes written in the dark by the women ‘computers’ of the1900s—not just star magnitudes, but little remarks like‘clouds interfered at 2 a.m., fingers freezing’. It makesscience intensely human.\nInterviewer: You’ve also experienceda major scare. Last spring, a minor earth tremor shook theobservatory hill.\nHazel: Yes, a magnitude-three tremor atdawn. Because our steel shelving racks had rubber seismicmounts, not a single glass plate cracked. The observatorydirector congratulated me at morning coffee and said ourprecautions had passed with flying colours. Everyone expectedme to feel proud and relieved. In reality, my blood rancold when I unlocked the vault: thirty heavy wooden boxeson the top shelf had slid forward until they were overhangingthe metal lip by three centimetres—one brief aftershockwould have sent three thousand plates crashing to thefloor.\nInterviewer: What did you do immediately afterthat?\nHazel: We didn’t move the archive off the hill, andwe haven’t stopped researchers handling original plates.Instead, we’ve fitted spring-loaded steel restraining barsacross the front of every shelf bay and moved all unboxedheavy plates to the lowest two tiers.",
