        "tapescript": "PART 1 – (Questions 1–5) A woman speaking to parent volunteers about a school summer fair\nWoman: Right, everyone, if I could have your attention for a few minutes. Thanks for coming. I’d like to run through the main points for this year’s summer fair.\nFirst, the date. You may remember that the newsletter said Saturday the twelfth, but the marquee company can’t deliver until the Friday after, so we’ve had to move everything back a week. That makes it Saturday the nineteenth. A couple of you suggested the Sunday instead, but the church hall, where we keep the stalls overnight, is booked for a wedding that day, so Saturday it is.\nNow, money, which is what this is all about. Last year we aimed to raise three thousand pounds and finished about eight hundred short. Ticket sales brought in eleven hundred and fifty, which was roughly what we’d expected from the adult tickets. The trouble is, we’d hoped the family tickets would take off, and they just didn’t. I know some of you would like to push the prices up, but after the complaints we had last time, I don’t think that’s on the table. And scrapping the family ticket would simply keep people away. So whatever we do about tickets, it’s what happens inside the gates that will decide whether we hit the target, and that’s where I’d like us to put our energy this year.\nWhich brings me to the games. Pete’s offering to run the bottle ring toss again. For anyone who hasn’t seen it, you throw a wooden ring and try to land it around the neck of a bottle. It sounds easy, but the difficulty isn’t the distance, as we only stand people three metres back, and nobody’s racing against the clock either. The real problem is that the rings are barely wider than the bottle necks, so they bounce off at the slightest touch. You really do need a steady hand. Pete tells me the children adore it, and I think that’s mainly because he always hands out a sticker or a balloon to the ones who miss, so nobody walks away with nothing. Mind you, it isn’t the cheapest game: it’s fifty pence a throw, the same as the other stalls. And there’s always a queue, so we’ll need two people on that stall.\nLast year the stall brought in two hundred pounds after we’d taken off fifty for the prizes, so it’s well worth repeating. Right, that’s all from me on the games. Does anyone have any questions before we move on to the raffle?",
