        "tapescript": "PART 2 – (Questions 6–10) A radio report about an autonomouspollination drone system called RoboBee\nPresenter: Welcome to‘Farming Tomorrow’. Today we visit the Netherlands, whereresearchers at AgriTech Delft have just completed a commercialtrial of RoboBee—a fleet of palm-sized drones designed topollinate tomato and strawberry crops inside giantglasshouses.\nLead engineer Dr Arthur Vermaas explains thatmany outsiders misinterpret the aim of the project.\nDr ArthurVermaas: “When people see tiny drones hovering between vines,they assume we’re spraying biological pesticides or filmingripe fruit for robotic harvesters. Neither is true, and ofcourse drones don’t lower a grower’s heating bill. Our soleobjective was to guarantee uniform pollination right throughthe darkest winter months, when natural bumblebees becomesluggish under artificial LED lighting.”\nPresenter: Ratherthan brushing against every blossom, a RoboBee hovers fivecentimetres away and emits a calibrated pulse of air thatshakes pollen loose. Normally, the drones patrol row by row.However, optical cameras scan flower clusters in real time,and whenever more than forty percent of the blossoms on atruss have just opened, the drone slows to half speed anddelivers a double pulse to maximise fruit set.\nBecauselithium batteries are heavy, each drone can fly for onlytwelve minutes at a stretch. It therefore returns to amagnetic perch on the overhead heating pipe. That perch doesmore than recharge the battery in three minutes: it alsosterilises the drone’s carbon-fibre wings with ultravioletlight so plant viruses are never carried from one row toanother.\nSurprisingly, the breakthrough in wing design camefrom a Swiss watchmaking supplier with no prior links toagriculture or aviation. The company doesn’t manufacturecameras or lithium cells, but for generations it has stampedultra-thin, fatigue-resistant titanium hinges for luxurywatchbands—the exact mechanism needed for wings that flapthirty times a second without snapping.\nWill RoboBee soonhum inside every European glasshouse? Dr Vermaas counselspatience.\nDr Arthur Vermaas: “Growers are impressed by thetwenty-percent jump in winter yield, but cardboard hives ofbumblebees are still dirt cheap. Until we can halve the leaseprice of our charging docks, only high-value berry andseed-breeding nurseries will sign up.”",
