        "tapescript": "PART 1 – (Questions 1–5) A marine conservation officer speaking to coastal volunteers about a harbour open day\nMs Clara: Hello, everyone, and welcome to the lifeboat station. I’m delighted to see so many new faces for our annual Harbour Seal and Seabird Open Day.\nBefore I hand out your hi-vis jackets, please make a note of the revised date. Our coastal poster campaign originally advertised Sunday thetwenty-first—the twenty-first of September. However, the harbour master notified us that a giant offshore wind-turbine barge is being towed through the narrows that morning, so we’ve brought the event forward a day to Saturday the twentieth. A few volunteers suggested postponing it to Sunday the twenty-eighth, but thespring tides that weekend will submerge the lower slipway, so Saturday the twentieth it must be.\nNow, regarding our conservation appeal. We need three thousand five hundred pounds to equip our new marine-mammal rescue van. Last year we fell about six hundred pounds short. Donation buckets at the entrance raised nine hundred pounds, which was respectable. Some committee members wanted to make entry to the pier ticket-only, but the pier is a public right of way, so we legally cannot charge admission. Cutting back on our free educational leaflets would defeat our purpose, too. What last year’s debrief showed is that visitors are far more willing to part with cash when we offer interactive stalls along the quay, so those stalls will have to carry the extra weight this year.\nSeveral of you will be stationed on the Telescope Spotting Deck at the end of the breakwater. We have six high-powered scopes trained on the sandbank where the grey seals haul out. Visitors often think spotting them is hard because the sandbank is half a mile offshore, or because sea spray fogs up the lenses. In fact, our scopes have anti-fog coatings and thirty-times magnification. The real challenge is that the seals’ mottled grey coats blend seamlessly into the wet granite boulders at low tide, so untrained eyes look straight past them unless you point out a flipper moving.\nRight next to the deck is our Eco-Badge Making stall, which is always a huge hit with younger children. At eighty pence a badge, it’s not free, and there’s usually a ten-minute wait for the hand press. The reason children queue up so eagerly is that, even if their own drawing goes slightly crooked in the press, our volunteers let them press a second badge of a puffin or a seal pup to clip onto their coat, so every child leaves wearing two badges.\nThat little stall is a serious earner: last year it cleared two hundred and ten pounds in profit after we’d subtracted thirty pounds for the metal pin-backs and plastic film. Right, let’s assign your posts!",
