        "tapescript": "PART 3 – (Questions 11–15) An interview with Serena Marsh, anacoustic ecologist\nInterviewer: Serena Marsh, welcome. As anacoustic ecologist, you travel the world recording endangerednatural soundscapes before human noise drowns them out. Howdid you find your way into such a rare field?\nSerena: Byputting together two halves of my life that seemed completelyat odds. I completed a degree in field zoology, and thenspent three years working nights as a commercial soundengineer in a London recording studio. My biology professorsthought I’d abandoned science for pop music. Yet knowing howamphibians and birds communicate while understandingmicrophone polar patterns and signal-to-noise ratios turnedout to be the exact toolkit needed for bioacoustics.\nInterviewer: Film studios and meditation-app developers oftenlicense your recordings. Do you mind commercial companiesusing your work?\nSerena: Not in the slightest, though theycan be amusingly naive. A Hollywood sound editor once askedme for a recording of a ‘silent Scottish pine forest at dawn’with all the midges and wind removed—which would basically bean empty digital file! It takes patience to explain thatnature isn’t a sterile studio. Still, licensing tracks tocinema and sleep apps brings in steady royalties, and thatincome pays for our solar-powered recorders in the Amazon.Without them, we’d be dependent on unpredictable academicgrants.\nInterviewer: What’s the most magical part of anexpedition for you?\nSerena: People picture me trekkingthrough dramatic mountain passes or spotting rare leopards oncamera traps. Honestly, hauling thirty kilos of batteriesthrough mud is exhausting. The moment that gives me goosebumpsis back in my tent at midnight, putting on headphones tocheck a spectrogram and discovering an ultrasonic chorus ofinsects and bats that the human ear couldn’t hear at allwhile standing in the clearing.\nInterviewer: Not every tripgoes smoothly. Last year, you lost three weeks of recordingsin Borneo.\nSerena: Yes. Curious macaque monkeys worked outhow to unclip the waterproof latches on six of our tree-mountedrecorders and chewed through the memory-card slots. When weretrieved the units, the local rangers apologisedprofusely, and my research partners said it was just anunavoidable occupational hazard of rainforest work. Everyoneexpected me to laugh it off as a funny wildlife story. Intruth, I was deeply frustrated with my own complacency—I’dused plastic latches to save a few hundred grams in baggageweight.\nInterviewer: How has that incident changed yourfieldwork?\nSerena: We haven’t stopped deploying autonomousrecorders in primate habitats, and we certainly haven’tresorted to repellent sprays. Instead, every recorder nowtransmits compressed daily audio summaries via satellite toour base-camp laptop each evening, so even if a casing isdamaged on day twenty, the previous nineteen days of data arealready safely backed up.",
