        "tapescript": "PART 1 – (Questions 1–5) An observatory outreach coordinator speaking to stewards about a public stargazing night\nDr Arthur: Good evening, everyone. Thank you for coming up to the dome early. Our annual Perseid Meteor Shower Public Night is our biggest outreach event of the summer, so let’s go over the arrangements.\nFirst, the date. As you know, our website initially listed Tuesday the twelfth of August to coincide with the astronomical peak. However, the Met Office has forecast a thick band of frontal cloud for Tuesday night, so we’ve activated our backup slot on Wednesday the thirteenth. A few members suggested holding it on Monday the eleventh instead, but the moon won’t set until after midnight on Monday, which would wash out the fainter meteors, so Wednesday is definitely our best bet.\nNow, a word on our fundraising target for the new solar filter. Last summer we aimed for two thousand pounds but ended up four hundred pounds short. Online booking fees covered our insurance and hot-chocolate Urns, just as planned. Some of you proposed doubling the car-park charge, but that would only encourage visitors to park along the dark verges outside, which is a safety hazard. Nor do we want to charge families to look through the main dome telescope. What last year’s figures demonstrated is that visitors love buying practical astronomy kits to use on the lawn, so our courtyard activity tables will have to generate that extra four hundred pounds.\nFor those of you marshalling the Lawn Binocular Zone, please brief visitors carefully. We lend out twenty pairs of giant astronomy binoculars mounted on tripods. First-timers always assume it’s tricky because the focus wheel is stiff, or because the binoculars are too heavy to tilt. In truth, they’re counterbalanced on fluid heads and pre-focused to infinity. The actual snag is that the eyepieces have a very narrow field of view, so if a visitor bumps the tripod leg even slightly with their knee in the dark, the star cluster vanishes from sight and you have to re-align it for them.\nInside the warm-up tent, we’re running the Planisphere Star-Wheel table, which attracts a huge crowd of teenagers and parents. At three pounds a kit, it’s not our cheapest item, and assembling the rotating disc takes a good ten minutes. Why do families love it so much? Because our stewards show them how to mark their own birthday constellation in glow-in-the-dark ink on the rim, so they can still read it out on the pitch-black lawn without using a torch.\nFinancially, that table is a star performer: last August it brought in two hundred and seventy pounds in clear profit after we’d taken off ninety pounds for the printed cardboard discs and brass fasteners. Let’s hope for clear skies on Wednesday!",
