        "tapescript": "PART 3 – (Questions 11–15) An interview with Marcus Thorne,an ice-core paleoclimatologist\nInterviewer: Marcus Thorne,welcome. You drill kilometres down into polar ice sheets toanalyse ancient air bubbles trapped tens of thousands ofyears ago. How did you prepare for a career like that?\nMarcus: Through what my career adviser called a completedetour. After finishing my degree in atmospheric physics, Ispent two years working as an apprentice precision machinistin a Sheffield toolworks. My university friends thought I’dlost my ambition. But when your electromechanical drill jamstwo thousand metres down an Antarctic borehole and the nearestspare part is four thousand miles away, being able to mill anew brass gear on a lathe is just as important asunderstanding isotope ratios.\nInterviewer: Your ice-corearchive also attracts requests from luxury mineral-waterbrands and documentary crews. How do you handle commercialapproaches?\nMarcus: With a firm sense of humour. A beverageexecutive once offered us a small fortune for a tonne often-thousand-year-old glacier ice to melt into a limited-editionbottled water—which we obviously turned down flat! Televisioncrews can also take up precious lab time. However, chargingbroadcasters a facility fee to film our slicing room fundsthree doctoral fellowships every year, so I’m more thanwilling to let them set up their cameras.\nInterviewer:What’s the most rewarding moment of working with an icecore?\nMarcus: Most people think it’s the heroic moment outon the ice sheet when the drill pulls up the deepest cylinderof blue ice. Actually, out on the ice you’re just numb withcold and worried about the winch cable. My favourite momentis months later in our quiet laboratory, when we melt a sliceof ice in a vacuum flask and hear the faint pop and fizz ofprehistoric air escaping—air that last touched the sky duringthe Ice Age.\nInterviewer: You’ve faced crises, too. Threeyears ago, a cooling compressor failed in your main storagefacility.\nMarcus: Yes, during a summer heatwave. We hadBackup liquid-nitrogen tanks, so the temperature inside thevault rose by only two degrees—from minus fifty to minusforty-eight—and not a single core was lost. The universitypress office hailed it as a triumph of our backup systems, andcolleagues expected me to feel proud. Honestly, I feltthoroughly shaken. The only reason we noticed the compressorhad tripped was that a technician happened to pop in on aSaturday evening to fetch his forgotten cycling helmet; theautomated text-alert system had silently failed a weekearlier.\nInterviewer: What did you change after thatdiscovery?\nMarcus: We didn’t replace the nitrogen tanks, andwe haven’t hired round-the-clock security guards. But we havewired the vault’s temperature sensors into a ‘heartbeat’monitor that sends an active confirmation pulse every thirtyminutes; if my phone doesn’t receive that pulse, the alarmtriggers automatically.",
