        "tapescript": "PART 2 – (Questions 6–10) A radio report about a self-healingroad material called BioAsphalt\nPresenter: Welcome to‘Infrastructure Focus’. Every spring, motorists complain aboutpotholes caused by winter frosts. Today we look at BioAsphalt,a self-repairing road surface trialled on a busy highwayoutside Gothenburg by the Swedish research consortium NordicRoadLabs.\nMaterials scientist Dr George Lindqvist explainswhat the surface was designed to achieve.\nDr GeorgeLindqvist: “Drivers often ask whether our road melts snow oncontact or cuts tyre noise in half. I’m afraid it doesneither, and it looks just as black as ordinary tarmac. Whatcosts highway authorities billions is freeze-thaw damage: waterseeps into hairline crevices, freezes, and blasts open craters.Our goal was to seal those microscopic cracks automaticallybefore water can ever penetrate the roadbed.”\nPresenter:Mixed into the asphalt are millions of tiny porous pelletsfilled with recycled sunflower oil and dormant limestone-producingspores. Under normal traffic, the pellets remain intact.However, when heavy lorries cause a micro-crack to formaround a pellet, the capsule ruptures and releases the oil,softening the surrounding bitumen so it flows together andcloses the gap within forty-eight hours.\nIn addition to thecapsules, the asphalt contains short fibres of recycled steelslag. Those steel fibres serve a dual purpose: they reinforcethe surface against heavy axle loads, and when a maintenancevan drives over the road with an induction coil once a year,the fibres heat up gently and trigger a second round ofhealing.\nRemarkably, the technique for encapsulating thesunflower oil came from a Danish food-processing company. TheDanish firm has no background in civil engineering or steelrecycling; for decades, however, it has produced heat-stablealginate beads used to trap flavourings inside commercialbakery products—which proved ideal for surviving hot asphaltmixers.\nWill BioAsphalt soon be laid on every nationalhighway? Dr Lindqvist remains measured.\nDr George Lindqvist:“The surface lasts twice as long, but it adds fifteen percentto the initial paving cost. Until municipal procurement rulesallow contractors to bid on thirty-year lifecycle costs ratherthan the lowest upfront price, adoption will be gradual.”",
