        "tapescript": "PART 3 – (Questions 11–15) An interview with Libby Chen, amaritime archaeologist\nInterviewer: Libby Chen, welcome.You lead underwater excavations of centuries-old merchantshipwrecks off the European coast. It’s a demandingprofession—how did you qualify for it?\nLibby: By taking tworoutes that my family thought were completely incompatible.I did a degree in medieval economic history, and then wentstraight to Aberdeen to qualify as a commercial saturationdiver alongside North Sea oil-rig welders. At the time, mytutors thought I was mad. But maritime archaeology isn’t justswimming around old cannons: you have to decipher fifteenth-centurycustoms ledgers in an archive and then work safely at fortymetres depth in zero visibility.\nInterviewer: Commercialtreasure-hunting companies and private salvors operate in thesame waters. How do you deal with commercial offshorefirms?\nLibby: Well, we never work with treasure hunters whobreak up wrecks for gold coins. However, offshore wind-farmand cable-laying companies are a different story. They can beimpatient when their sonar hits an uncharted timber hull andthey have to pause pipe-laying for a week while we inspectit. Yet under maritime law, those developers pay for thehigh-resolution sonar surveys and conservation tanks that ouruniversity budget could never afford, so partnering with themis invaluable.\nInterviewer: What’s the most thrilling momentof a wreck excavation?\nLibby: Television viewers think it’slifting a bronze cannon or a chest of silver to the surface.Honestly, cannons are heavy, repetitive và a nightmare todesalt. What stops me in my tracks is opening a sealedstoneware jar or a sailor’s wooden sea-chest and findingsomething touchingly ordinary—a pair of knitted woollenmittens, peppercorns, or a wooden comb still bearing theowner’s carved nickname.\nInterviewer: Underwater workcarries real risks. Two autumns ago, a sudden storm hit yourresearch vessel in the Channel.\nLibby: Yes. A gale blew upfour hours earlier than forecast while we were winching afragile seventeenth-century oak rudder onto the deck. Thecrane cable snapped in the swell, and the rudder dropped backsix metres onto the sandy seabed. Miraculously, the sandcushioned the fall and the timber wasn’t even cracked. Theship’s captain said we’d been blessed with incredible luck,and the crew cheered when our camera confirmed it was intact.I didn’t feel lucky at all—I felt mortified that I’d letschedule pressure tempt me into starting a heavy lift with afalling barometer.\nInterviewer: What rule did you introduceafter that day?\nLibby: We haven’t stopped lifting largestructural timbers, and we still work in autumn. But I’vehanded absolute veto power over every crane lift to thevessel’s mate: if swell height exceeds one metre, noarchaeologist on board—including me—is allowed to authorisea recovery.",
