        "tapescript": "PART 2 – (Questions 6–10) A radio report about a smartstadium turf system called PitchPulse\nPresenter: Welcome to‘Sports Science Weekly’. Modern football and rugby stadiumswith high roofs often struggle to keep their grass pitchesintact through winter. Today we look at PitchPulse, anunder-soil root-management system developed by AlbionSportsTech and trialled at three Premier League grounds.\nLead agronomist Mr Ryan Gallagher explains the real purposebehind the technology.\nMr Ryan Gallagher: “Spectators assumea high-tech pitch is designed so clubs can stage rock concertswithout laying protective flooring, or so groundskeepers neverhave to mow or paint touchlines. None of that is true. Inwinter, shallow roots cause turf to tear loose under aplayer’s studs, leading to devastating knee ligament injuries.Our single goal was to anchor grass roots twenty centimetresdeep so the surface stays firm in torrential rain.”\nPresenter: Beneath the turf lies a web of biodegradablepolymer mesh and fibre-optic moisture sensors. Under normalconditions, the pitch is watered on a fixed schedule.However, whenever sensors detect that surface moisture in thegoalmouth has exceeded seventy percent two hours beforekick-off, vacuum pumps beneath the gravel bed switch onautomatically, drawing excess rainwater down through the rootzone in fifteen minutes.\nWhen the pumps aren’t drainingwater, they can be reversed to blow warm air gentlyupwards through the soil. That airflow achieves two thingsthroughout the winter: it keeps root-zone temperatures attwelve degrees so grass keeps growing, and it supplies oxygento beneficial soil microbes that prevent root rot.\nIntriguingly, the biodegradable mesh that the grass rootstwine around was created by an Italian medical-textile firm.The Italian company knew nothing about football pitches ordrainage pumps, but it specialises in weaving surgical herniameshes that dissolve harmlessly after natural tissue growsthrough them.\nWill lower-league clubs soon install PitchPulseat their grounds? Mr Gallagher is realistic.\nMr RyanGallagher: “Every club doctor wants a safer pitch, butexcavating a stadium bowl and laying vacuum pipes costs nearlytwo million pounds. Until league governing bodies offermatching grants for player-welfare infrastructure, onlytop-flight stadiums will be able to afford it.”",
