        "tapescript": "PART 3 – (Questions 11–15) An interview with Kate Lindholm, ascientific botanical illustrator\nInterviewer: Kate Lindholm,welcome. In an age of high-resolution digital photography,you earn your living painting pen-and-ink and watercolourillustrations of newly discovered plant species for botanicaljournals. How did you get into the field?\nKate: By combiningtwo disciplines that sounded like a contradiction in terms.I did a degree in plant taxonomy, and then trained for ayear in surgical and anatomical illustration at a teachinghospital. My botany friends couldn’t understand why I wassketching tendons and bone joints. But medical illustrationteaches you to measure structures under a dissectingmicroscope to a tenth of a millimetre and show overlappinglayers with absolute clarity—which a camera lens, with itsshallow depth of field, still can’t do.\nInterviewer:Alongside scientific journals, luxury perfume houses andartisanal tea brands commission artwork from you. Do youenjoy commercial packaging work?\nKate: I do, though artdirectors can test your scientific conscience! One perfumebrand asked me to paint a rare Madagascar vanilla orchidwith eight petals instead of six because it looked ‘moresymmetrical’ on the bottle—I had to gently refuse and showthem how to arrange six petals gracefully. Still, commercialcommissions pay ten times what an academic journal canoffer, and that income allows me to illustrate new speciesfor botanists in developing countries at no charge.\nInterviewer: What’s the most satisfying part of working on anew plant illustration?\nKate: People assume it’s seeing thefinished colour plate framed in a gallery exhibition. To behonest, by the time a painting is framed, I only notice thetiny brushstrokes I wish I’d done differently. The moment Itruly cherish is when I soak a flattened, brown driedherbarium specimen in warm water, unfold a petal under mymicroscope, and suddenly work out the exact three-dimensionalhinge that allows the flower to trap its pollinator.\nInterviewer: Have you ever had a disaster in the studio?\nKate: Oh, yes. Four years ago, while I was away for theweekend, a skylight seal leaked during a thunderstorm anddripped rainwater onto my drawing board, ruining three weeksof ink work on a new Peruvian fern. The botanist who’dcommissioned it was wonderfully kind and extended thepublication deadline by a month, and my husband said at leastthe pressed plant specimen itself hadn’t been touched.Everyone expected me to feel grateful that the specimen wassafe. Instead, I was mortified by my own carelessness—I’dleft a month’s original artwork lying flat on an open deskunder a roof window.\nInterviewer: What habit did you changeafter that weekend?\nKate: I haven’t moved studios, and Istill work in traditional waterproof India ink rather thanswitching to a digital tablet. However, at the end of everysingle working day, I now scan the unfinished drawing boardat twelve hundred DPI and lock the physical sheet inside asteel plan-chest before I switch off the light.",
