        "tapescript": "PART 2 – (Questions 6–10) A radio report about anavalanche-detection radar network called SnowGuard\nPresenter: Good morning. In the Swiss and Austrian Alps,winter avalanches regularly threaten mountain railways. Todaywe examine SnowGuard, an automated Doppler-radar systemdeveloped by Alpine Sentinel that has just completed itssecond winter protecting the Bernina mountain line.\nLeadengineer Dr Victoria Brandt explains what sets SnowGuardapart.\nDr Victoria Brandt: “People often confuse our radarunits with explosive canons that trigger artificial avalanches,or they think the system predicts seasonal snowfall months inadvance. We don’t trigger slides, and we don’t clear snow offthe rails. Our single critical task is to spot a snowpackfracturing two kilometres up the mountain face and switch therailway signals to red within four seconds, halting oncomingtrains safely outside the avalanche path.”\nPresenter: Eachsolar-powered radar mast scans the high ridges continuously.During light snowfall, it operates in a low-power standbymode. However, ground geophones monitor vibrations in thebedrock, and whenever a tremor indicates that a slab of snowwider than ten metres has shifted, the radar switches tohigh-frequency tracking and locks onto the moving mass.\nOperating at three thousand metres altitude means ice canquickly coat the radar dish. SnowGuard solves this with ahydrophobic fluoropolymer dome over the antenna. The domeperforms two jobs throughout the winter: it sheds wet snowand freezing rain before ice can build up, and it focuses theradar beam so false alarms from blowing powder are filteredout.\nSurprisingly, the dome material was developed by aFinnish telecommunications firm that had never worked inavalanche safety. The company doesn’t build train brakes orseismic geophones, but it specialises in ice-shedding radomecovers for Arctic coastal lighthouses.\nWill SnowGuard soonguard every mountain road and ski resort in Europe? Dr Brandtis realistic.\nDr Victoria Brandt: “Railway companies canjustify the investment because trains run on fixed tracks.Until our new wide-angle array can cover winding multi-lanehighway passes with a single mast, most regional roaddepartments will stick with traditional snow sheds.”",
